Yeah. I was looking at what a new interface does to one person. You’re looking at how a society takes on a different personality even while most of the people in it carry on with the same basic needs, fears, and loyalties.

And the big zeitgeist can change before most people change their minds. Imagine a workplace where everybody privately thinks staying late is pointless, but the boss keeps praising whoever’s there at seven. From the outside it looks like a culture of ambition. Then a new boss starts promoting people who get good work done and go home. Within a month the same office feels like a different place. The private opinions barely moved. What people could do openly, and what earned them respect, changed almost overnight.

Now scale that up. A run club treats waking up at five as discipline. A group of night-shift nurses treats protecting your sleep as discipline. Both are little zeitgeists with their own standards of what counts as admirable. The bigger zeitgeist emerges partly from which of those worlds gets to set the terms outside its own circle. And once one does, people in the other worlds start explaining themselves in its language.

That’s where your DNA analogy gets interesting. A culture can carry an attitude for decades in pockets where it’s ordinary. Then attention, permission, and reward line up, and suddenly that attitude looks like the defining trait of an era. It was there the whole time. And once it’s visible, people can organize around it, build institutions that reward it, and raise kids who experience it as common sense. What began as a change in expression becomes part of the environment that shapes the next generation.

Matthew Perry’s public identity was compressed around Friends. An era gets compressed too, while it’s still happening. The difference is that the people inside the era can see the portrait taking shape and start acting like the people in it.